1
00:00:00,119 --> 00:00:05,894
What if your program could make a
decision, like printing pass or fail,
depending on a score?

2
00:00:06,234 --> 00:00:10,985
That's exactly what if and else do, and
they're the reason programs feel smart.

3
00:00:11,325 --> 00:00:18,977
I'm Mina, this is Code in Small Steps, and
by the end of this video, you'll write a
grade checker that handles four different
grades.

4
00:00:21,917 --> 00:00:25,596
An if statement asks a question that's
either true or false.

5
00:00:25,935 --> 00:00:32,551
If the answer is true, Python runs the
indented code underneath. If it's false,
Python skips it.

6
00:00:32,891 --> 00:00:37,217
Else is the backup plan. It runs only when
the condition was false.

7
00:00:37,556 --> 00:00:44,014
It's like the gate at a cinema. If you
have a ticket, you go in. Else, you go to
the ticket counter.

8
00:00:44,355 --> 00:00:45,525
Let's see it in code.

9
00:00:46,144 --> 00:00:47,822
Here's a score of seventy two.

10
00:00:48,322 --> 00:00:53,145
If the score is at least forty, print
pass. Otherwise, print fail.

11
00:00:53,645 --> 00:00:57,665
Run it, and we get pass, because seventy
two is at least forty.

12
00:00:58,165 --> 00:01:03,174
Change the score to thirty five and run it
again. Now the else branch runs instead.

13
00:01:07,274 --> 00:01:11,025
Notice the four spaces before print.
They're not decoration.

14
00:01:11,525 --> 00:01:15,427
In Python, indentation tells it which
lines belong to the if.

15
00:01:15,927 --> 00:01:20,801
Delete the spaces and run it, and Python
refuses, with an indentation error.

16
00:01:21,301 --> 00:01:26,996
It even tells you why: it expected an
indented block after the if statement on
line two.

17
00:01:27,496 --> 00:01:29,982
Put the spaces back, and it runs again.

18
00:01:34,082 --> 00:01:38,921
Pass or fail is fine, but real grades have
more levels. That's what elif is for.

19
00:01:39,421 --> 00:01:44,448
Elif means, else if. Python checks each
condition from top to bottom.

20
00:01:44,948 --> 00:01:52,335
Seventy two isn't ninety or more, and it
isn't seventy five or more, but it is
forty or more. So we get a C.

21
00:01:52,835 --> 00:01:57,998
And here's the important part. Python
stops at the first condition that's true.

22
00:01:58,498 --> 00:02:03,476
Watch what happens if I put the forty
check first, and use a score of ninety
five.

23
00:02:03,976 --> 00:02:11,162
Ninety five should be an A, but we get a
C, because forty or more was true first,
and Python never looked any further.

24
00:02:11,662 --> 00:02:14,638
So always check the strictest condition
first.

25
00:02:18,458 --> 00:02:20,582
Conditions usually compare two values.

26
00:02:20,802 --> 00:02:20,802
Double equals checks if two things are
equal. An exclamation mark and equals
checks if they're different.

27
00:02:20,921 --> 00:02:24,720
And you've got less than, greater than,
and their or-equal versions.

28
00:02:25,060 --> 00:02:30,151
The most common mistake is using a single
equals sign inside an if. Let me show you.

29
00:02:30,771 --> 00:02:34,486
Here I wrote, if score equals forty, with
one equals sign.

30
00:02:34,986 --> 00:02:40,591
Python stops with a syntax error, and the
message is brilliant. Maybe you meant
double equals?

31
00:02:41,091 --> 00:02:45,713
Remember, one equals sign stores a value.
Two equals signs compare.

32
00:02:46,213 --> 00:02:48,881
Add the second equals sign, and it works.

33
00:02:52,981 --> 00:02:57,453
One last tool. You can combine conditions,
with and, and or.

34
00:02:57,953 --> 00:03:04,708
With and, both sides must be true. Here
the age is twenty, and has I D is true, so
entry is allowed.

35
00:03:05,208 --> 00:03:10,438
Set has I D to false, and the whole
condition becomes false, even though the
age is fine.

36
00:03:10,938 --> 00:03:17,869
With or, only one side needs to be true.
That's handy for checks like, if the day
is Saturday, or Sunday.

37
00:03:21,969 --> 00:03:25,243
Let's put it all together, with a cinema
ticket price.

38
00:03:25,743 --> 00:03:33,483
Children under five go free. Anyone under
eighteen, or any student, pays a hundred.
Everyone else pays two hundred.

39
00:03:33,983 --> 00:03:39,885
Our viewer is fifteen, so the first check
is false, but the elif is true. One
hundred.

40
00:03:40,385 --> 00:03:43,066
Now let's try an adult who isn't a
student.

41
00:03:43,566 --> 00:03:48,312
Both sides of the or are false, so we fall
through to else, and pay two hundred.

42
00:03:48,812 --> 00:03:54,555
The order, the or, and the else all work
together. That's most of what you need for
real decisions.

43
00:03:56,055 --> 00:04:05,336
Your turn. Store an age, and print can
vote if it's eighteen or more, and not yet
otherwise. Pause the video, or try it
during this timer.

44
00:04:16,836 --> 00:04:17,996
Here's one solution.

45
00:04:18,496 --> 00:04:21,279
With seventeen, it prints, not yet.

46
00:04:21,779 --> 00:04:28,231
Change it to eighteen, and it prints, can
vote, because greater than or equal
includes eighteen itself.

47
00:04:29,449 --> 00:04:35,405
Let's recap. If runs code only when a
condition is true, and else catches
everything else.

48
00:04:35,743 --> 00:04:42,229
Elif adds more choices, and Python stops
at the first true one, so put the
strictest check first.

49
00:04:42,570 --> 00:04:48,111
Indentation decides what belongs to the
if. And in conditions, compare with double
equals.

50
00:04:48,450 --> 00:04:55,335
If you got stuck anywhere, rewatch that
chapter, then change the numbers and
predict the output before you run it.

51
00:04:55,673 --> 00:05:03,921
Next time, we'll make code repeat itself
with loops. Subscribe so you don't miss
it, and I'll see you in the next small
step.
